READ THIS FIRST — this is a placeholder you are meant to choose, not a decision
that has been made for you.

"All rights reserved" is the default here because it is the only one that can be
taken back. You can relicense your own work as MIT, GPL or anything else at any
time; you cannot un-MIT something once a copy is out in the world. So this
starts closed, and opening it is a one-line change when you have decided.

Every third-party component Cadmium uses is permissive (MIT, Apache-2.0, OFL) —
see THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md — so none of them force a choice on you. Selling
Cadmium, giving it away, or opening the source are all allowed by all of them.

To open it instead, replace everything in this file from "Terms" down with the
text of the licence you want, and say so in README.md. The usual candidates:

  MIT          — anyone may do anything, including sell a closed fork.
  GPL-3.0      — anyone may do anything, but a distributed fork must also be
                 GPL-3.0 with source. Note that this would NOT stop someone
                 hosting it as a service.
  AGPL-3.0     — as GPL-3.0, and network use counts as distribution.
  Business Source / "source available" — source published, commercial use
                 restricted for N years, then converts to an open licence.

Whatever you pick, keep THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md shipping alongside it: those
obligations are separate from this file and do not go away.
